n all three regions (us-east, us-west, eu-west):\n\n| Service | Current (bad) | Status | Last healthy in production |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| payments-api | v5.1.0 | degraded | v5.0.0 |\n| payments-worker | v3.4.0 | unhealthy | v3.3.0 |\n\nEach one's latest release is the bad one, and the release before it was healthy. The deploy history doesn't say which one went out this morning. The matching error rates suggest one may be breaking the other, so rolling back the wrong one could leave the real cause in place.\n\nWhich do you want?\n1. Roll back **payments-api** to v5.0.0\n2. Roll back **payments-worker** to v3.3.0\n3. Roll back **both**\n\nI'll open the SEV2 incident at the same time, against whichever service you pick, or naming both if it's option 3. Neither rollback needs change-manager approval, because both versions were healthy in production before.", "type": "text"}]}, {"stop_reason": "tool_use", "content": [{"id": "toolu_01FkZsf5Woxu8NwN9bpkRaLn", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"service": "payments-worker", "version": "v3.3.0", "confidence": 0.95}, "name": "rollback", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}, {"id": "toolu_012mfSJPSSuwnFNAmi8PAihT", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"title": "payments-worker v3.4.0 errors after deploy; rolled back to v3.3.0", "severity": "SEV2", "service": "payments-worker", "summary": "Since this morning's deploy of payments-worker v3.4.0, the service is unhealthy with ~18% error rate across us-east, us-west and eu-west. payments-api (v5.1.0) shows the same error rate, believed to be downstream of the worker. Rolled payments-worker back to v3.3.0, its most recent release that was healthy in production. payments-api not changed; check that it recovers once the worker is back on v3.3.0.", "idempotency_key": "payments-worker-v3.4.0-rollback-sev2", "confidence": 0.93}, "name": "create_incident", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "tool_use", "content": [{"citations": null, "text": "Let me check whether things are recovering.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "toolu_01BSFZHU5yV91Cm2PmefXff3", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"service": "payments-worker", "confidence": 0.9}, "name": "get_service_status", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}, {"id": "toolu_01AFTHhR9NmCtsnvrRD66xX1", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"service": "payments-api", "confidence": 0.9}, "name": "get_service_status", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "end_turn", "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "", "type": "thinking"}, {"citations": null, "text": "I rolled payments-worker back to v3.3.0 and opened the SEV2. The worker has recovered, but payments-api hasn't yet.\n\n- **Rollback:** payments-worker went from v3.4.0 to v3.3.0, its most recent release that was healthy in production, in all three regions. It now reports healthy, with errors down from 18% to 0.2%.\n- **Incident:** I opened INC-0001 as a SEV2 against payments-worker, and the on-call engineer has been paged. The summary says the API errors are believed to be downstream of the worker.\n- **Still failing:** payments-api is still at 18% errors on v5.1.0. It may just need a little time to recover. But v5.1.0 is also marked \"degraded\" in the API's own deploy history, so it may have a problem of its own.\n\nI haven't changed payments-api. If it doesn't recover in the next few minutes, rolling it back to v5.0.0 (its last healthy version) is an option. I can do that if you want, or check its status again first.", "type": "text"}]}]}